# Normalise line endings in the repository, whatever the checkout platform.
#
# Without this, Git on Windows rewrites text files to CRLF on checkout, and the
# committed bytes depend on who committed last. That matters here because CI
# runs on Linux: a file that is CRLF in the index but expected to be LF can
# change lint and formatter results between a local run and CI.
#
# `text=auto` stores LF in the repository and lets Git choose LF on checkout for
# text files, so what CI reads is what was committed.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows-only scripts must keep CRLF to run under cmd.exe.
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Binary files are never touched.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.webp binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.pdf binary
*.tgz binary

# Keep lockfiles byte-stable: a line-ending rewrite makes a spurious diff that
# hides real dependency changes.
pnpm-lock.yaml -text
